<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of New Hampshire</h1><p class="st-meta">1629 CE – 1776 CE · capital Portsmouth</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The small northern New England colony around Portsmouth, a source of masts for the Royal Navy, and the first colony to adopt its own constitution in 1776.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Fishing settlements at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dover-new-hampshire">Dover</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-new-hampshire">Portsmouth</a>, then called Strawbery Banke, appeared on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piscataqua-river">Piscataqua River</a> from 1623. In 1629 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-mason-governor">John Mason</a> received a grant of the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merrimack-river">Merrimack</a> and the Piscataqua and named it after his home county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter-new-hampshire">Exeter</a> was founded in 1638 by the dissenting minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wheelwright">John Wheelwright</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massachusetts-bay-colony">Massachusetts Bay Colony</a> governed the towns from 1641 until the Crown made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> a royal province in 1679. It shared a governor with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-massachusetts-bay">Province of Massachusetts Bay</a> from 1698 to 1741. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benning-wentworth">Benning Wentworth</a>, governor from 1741 to 1766, it issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire-grants">New Hampshire Grants</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a>. Its economy rested on lumber, white-pine masts reserved for the navy, shipbuilding and fishing.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Royal governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wentworth-governor">John Wentworth</a> fled in 1775. In January 1776 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> adopted a temporary constitution, the first of any colony, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-declaration-of-independence">Declaration of Independence</a> as a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-new-hampshire.jpg" alt="Province of New Hampshire" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: AnonMoos, based on image by Zscout370, AnonMoos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_ensign_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800,_square_canton).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1629 CE – 1776 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Portsmouth</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Mason, Benning Wentworth, John Wentworth</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Congregationalism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Hampshire (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1702&amp;focus=province-of-new-hampshire" aria-label="Province of New Hampshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2973 648 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of New Hampshire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3293 904L3283 922M3293 904L4970 663M3293 904L3277 927M3293 904L4997 651M3293 904L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3283 922h0M4970 663h0M3277 927h0M4997 651h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3293 904h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3293 904h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Portsmouth. <a href="/atlas?year=1702&amp;focus=province-of-new-hampshire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
